const LEARN = [
{id:'what',title:'What is a Divya Desam?',html:`
<p>A Divya Desam is a temple of Vishnu praised in the hymns of the Azhwars, twelve poet-saints of the Tamil country who lived roughly between the sixth and ninth centuries. Their four thousand verses, the Nalayira Divya Prabandham, are honoured by Sri Vaishnavas as the Tamil Veda. The act of singing the Lord's welfare at a shrine is called <b>mangalasasanam</b>.</p>
<p>There are 108: 105 in India, one in Nepal, and two beyond this world, the Milk Ocean and Paramapadam. So a pilgrim can walk to <b>106</b>. The list was fixed in the form we know by later teachers, notably in the <i>Nootrettu Thiruppathi Andadi</i> of Pillai Perumal Iyengar.</p>
<p>The verses were nearly lost. In the ninth or tenth century Nathamuni heard ten verses on the Lord of Kumbakonam, went looking for the rest, and received all four thousand from Nammazhwar in meditation at Azhwar Thirunagari. He set them to music and placed them in temple worship, where they are still sung.</p>
<p>The regions follow the old Tamil kingdoms: Chola Nadu (40), Nadu Nadu (2), Thondai Nadu (22), Malai Nadu (13), Pandya Nadu (18), Vada Nadu in the north (11) and Vinnulagam, the heavenly realm (2).</p>`},
{id:'sv',title:'Sri Vaishnavism in simple words',html:`
<p><b>Who is God?</b> Sriman Narayana, always with Sri (Lakshmi). The name of the tradition begins with Her because She is the mediator (purushakara) who recommends the soul to the Lord and softens His justice with mercy.</p>
<p><b>What is the philosophy?</b> Visishtadvaita, taught by Ramanujacharya (1017 to 1137). Souls and matter are real and eternal, and they are the body of God, who is their inner soul. There is one reality, qualified by these distinctions.</p>
<p><b>What is the path?</b> Bhakti, and above all <b>prapatti</b> or saranagati: giving oneself entirely into the Lord's care. Its support is three "secrets": the eight-syllable mantra, the dvaya mantra and the carama-sloka (Bhagavad-gita 18.66).</p>
<p><b>Five forms of the Lord:</b> para (in Vaikuntha), vyuha (in the Milk Ocean), vibhava (the avataras), antaryami (in the heart) and <b>archa</b> (the deity in the temple). The archa form is prized most because it is the one within reach of everyone, which is why the Divya Desams matter so much.</p>
<p><b>Teachers to know:</b> Nathamuni, Yamunacharya (Alavandar), Ramanuja, Pillai Lokacharya, Vedanta Desika and Manavala Mamunigal.</p>`},
{id:'kalai',title:'Tenkalai and Vadakalai',html:`
<p>About two centuries after Ramanuja two schools took shape. They share the same Lord, scriptures, acharya and temples. The differences are of emphasis, traditionally counted as eighteen points. Both are respected here; please ask the local devotees which tradition a temple follows.</p>
<table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Tenkalai (southern)</th><th>Vadakalai (northern)</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Chief teachers</td><td>Pillai Lokacharya, Manavala Mamunigal</td><td>Vedanta Desika</td></tr>
<tr><td>Grace and effort</td><td>Grace is unconditioned. The Lord carries the soul as a mother cat carries her kitten.</td><td>Grace responds to a gesture of surrender. The soul holds on as a baby monkey clings to its mother.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Prapatti</td><td>Not an act that earns anything; it is the recognition that He alone is the means.</td><td>A definite act with limbs, performed once, which the Lord has chosen to accept as the occasion for His grace.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sri (Lakshmi)</td><td>Supreme mediator; a soul of unique status, not infinite in the way the Lord is.</td><td>Co-equal with the Lord, infinite, and Herself able to grant liberation.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Scripture</td><td>Strong emphasis on the Tamil Prabandham.</td><td>Sanskrit Veda and Tamil Prabandham given equal weight (Ubhaya Vedanta is shared by both).</td></tr>
<tr><td>The Lord and the sinner</td><td>He relishes the faults of the surrendered soul as a cow licks her newborn calf.</td><td>He forgives the faults; He does not relish them.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Tilaka</td><td>Y-shaped, with a foot extending onto the nose.</td><td>U-shaped, without the foot.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Seats today</td><td>Srirangam, Vanamamalai, Azhwar Thirunagari, Sriperumbudur, Tirumala jeeyars.</td><td>Ahobila Mutt, Parakala Mutt, Andavan Ashramams; Kanchi Varadaraja temple.</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p>A simple way to hold it: Tenkalai guards the truth that mercy is causeless; Vadakalai guards the truth that the soul must turn to receive it. A Gaudiya will find both in his own tradition: <i>krsna-krpa</i> is causeless, and yet <i>saranagati</i> has six limbs.</p>`},
{id:'syn',title:'Sri Vaishnava and Gaudiya siddhanta: a synthesis',html:`
<p class="note">This section is written for Gaudiya devotees visiting Sri Vaishnava temples. It tries to state each side as its own teachers would. Please correct it through the feedback button.</p>
<h4>What the two share</h4>
<ul>
<li>Vishnu is the Supreme Person, with form, qualities and pastimes. Impersonal monism is rejected by both.</li>
<li>The soul is an eternal individual and by nature a servant of God.</li>
<li>Bhakti and surrender, not knowledge or ritual alone, bring the soul to Him.</li>
<li>The deity in the temple is the Lord Himself, not a symbol.</li>
<li>Service to the devotees is as dear to Him as service to Himself, or dearer.</li>
<li>The six limbs of surrender are common property. Gaudiya texts quote the same verse (<i>anukulyasya sankalpah</i>) from the Vaishnava-tantra.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Where they differ</h4>
<table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Sri Vaishnava</th><th>Gaudiya Vaishnava</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Vedanta</td><td>Visishtadvaita: souls and matter are the body of Brahman.</td><td>Acintya-bhedabheda: souls and matter are energies of the Lord, inconceivably one with and different from Him.</td></tr>
<tr><td>The original form</td><td>Narayana in Vaikuntha is the source; Krishna and Rama are His full avataras.</td><td>Krishna is svayam bhagavan (Srimad-Bhagavatam 1.3.28; Brahma-samhita 5.1); Narayana is His vilasa form in majesty.</td></tr>
<tr><td>The Goddess</td><td>Sri, Bhu and Nila; Andal is Bhu Devi.</td><td>Srimati Radharani is the source; Lakshmi is Her expansion in Vaikuntha.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Chief mood</td><td>Servant to master, with awe and intimacy mixed. The Azhwars also sing as mother and as beloved.</td><td>Five rasas, with the unreserved sweetness (madhurya) of Vraja held highest.</td></tr>
<tr><td>The goal</td><td>Eternal service (kainkarya) to the Divine Couple in Vaikuntha.</td><td>Loving service in Goloka Vrindavana under the gopis and their followers.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Main practice</td><td>Prapatti, temple service, recitation of the Prabandham, the three mantras.</td><td>Congregational chanting of the Holy Name, hearing Bhagavatam, deity worship.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Prime texts</td><td>Divya Prabandham, Sri Bhashya, Bhagavad-vishayam.</td><td>Srimad-Bhagavatam, Caitanya-caritamrta, the Gosvamis' works.</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<h4>The conversation at Srirangam</h4>
<p>Caitanya-caritamrta (Madhya 9) records the friendly exchange between Mahaprabhu and Venkata Bhatta. The Lord asked, smiling: Lakshmi is the most chaste, so why did She perform austerity to join Krishna's rasa dance? And why did She not succeed, when the gopis did? Venkata Bhatta answered that Narayana and Krishna are one, so Her chastity is not broken. Mahaprabhu agreed, and added that Krishna in Vraja is approached only by following the gopis' mood, without the sense of His majesty. He concluded without a trace of rivalry: there is no difference between the forms of the Lord; the difference is in the rasa.</p>
<h4>How a Gaudiya pilgrim can enter a Divya Desam</h4>
<ul>
<li>See Sriman Narayana as the same Krishna in His aspect of majesty, and Thayar as the merciful Mother who presents you to Him. Go to Her shrine first where that is the custom.</li>
<li>Listen for Vraja in the Azhwars. Periyazhwar is Yasoda. Andal is a gopi. Nammazhwar pines as a lover in a third of his verses. Kulasekhara rocks Rama to sleep.</li>
<li>Honour the debt. Srila Prabhupada called Ramanujacharya a great acharya of the Vaishnava world; the Sri sampradaya is one of the four authorised lines; Gopala Bhatta Gosvami was born in a Srirangam priest's house; Jiva Gosvami cites Ramanuja with respect; and Hari-bhakti-vilasa draws on South Indian temple practice.</li>
<li>Follow local rules on dress, queues and what may be touched. Take sathari, tirtha and prasadam with gratitude.</li>
</ul>`},
{id:'route',title:'Sri Caitanya Mahaprabhu and Nityananda Prabhu at the Divya Desams',html:`
<p>Mahaprabhu toured South India from 1510 to 1512. Caitanya-caritamrta, Madhya-lila chapter 9, lists the holy places. Those that are Divya Desams, in the order of the text:</p>
<table><thead><tr><th>In the text</th><th>Divya Desam</th><th>Reference</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Ahovala-nrisimha</td><td>105 Ahobilam</td><td>Madhya 9.16 to 17</td></tr>
<tr><td>Tirupati, Tirumala, Venkata hill</td><td>106 Thiruvenkadam</td><td>Madhya 9.64 to 65</td></tr>
<tr><td>Vishnu-kanchi</td><td>43 Varadaraja, Kanchipuram</td><td>Madhya 9.69 to 70</td></tr>
<tr><td>Kumbhakarna-kapala</td><td>Kumbakonam town (12)</td><td>Madhya 9.78</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sri Ranga-kshetra</td><td>1 Srirangam</td><td>Madhya 9.79 to 165</td></tr>
<tr><td>Rishabha Hill</td><td>91 Azhagar Koil (some say; the purport says the Palni hills)</td><td>Madhya 9.166 to 173</td></tr>
<tr><td>Durvasana, temple of Ramacandra</td><td>Possibly 94 Thiruppullani (the purport places it near Ramnad)</td><td>Madhya 9.198</td></tr>
<tr><td>Naya-tripati on the Tamraparni</td><td>87 Azhwar Thirunagari and the Nava Tirupati</td><td>Madhya 9.219</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sri Vaikuntha</td><td>80 Srivaikuntam</td><td>Madhya 9.222</td></tr>
<tr><td>Adi-kesava (Brahma-samhita found)</td><td>76 Thiruvattar</td><td>Madhya 9.234 to 241</td></tr>
<tr><td>Ananta Padmanabha</td><td>75 Thiruvananthapuram</td><td>Madhya 9.241 to 242</td></tr>
<tr><td>Mathura and Gokula</td><td>102, 103</td><td>Madhya 17 and 18</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p class="note">Verse numbers were checked against the Bhaktivedanta Book Trust text of Madhya-lila chapter 9. The conversation with Venkata Bhatta on Lakshmi and the gopis runs from 9.111 to 9.162.</p>
<p>The text names other places in the Pandya country, such as Panagadi, Chiyadatala and Gajendra-mokshana, whose identification with particular Divya Desams is discussed by scholars and is left open here.</p>
<p><b>Sri Nityananda Prabhu</b> wandered the holy places for twenty years as a young avadhuta. Caitanya-bhagavata, Adi-khanda chapter 9, names among them Ayodhya, Naimisaranya, Mathura and Gokula, Dvaraka, Badarikasrama, Venkatanatha, Kanchi and Sri Ranga on the Kaveri.</p>`},
{id:'tips',title:'Yatra with family: practical notes',html:`
<ul>
<li><b>Hours.</b> Most Tamil Nadu temples open about 6 am to 12 noon and 4 pm to 8.30 pm. Kerala temples open earlier and close by 11 am. Small village temples may open only when the priest is called; keep the phone number from the notice board.</li>
<li><b>Dress.</b> Dhoti or pyjama-kurta for men, sari or salwar for women is accepted everywhere. In Kerala and at Thiruvattar men remove the shirt.</li>
<li><b>Season.</b> November to February is best in the south. Margazhi (mid-December to mid-January) is the month of Thiruppavai and Vaikuntha Ekadasi. The Himalayan shrines are open about May to November.</li>
<li><b>With elders.</b> Plan three or four temples a day, not eight. Sholinghur, Ahobilam upper shrines, Thiruneermalai hilltop and Muktinath involve climbing or altitude.</li>
<li><b>Order of darshan.</b> In many Sri Vaishnava temples one sees Garuda, then Thayar, then the Lord. In practice, follow the queue.</li>
<li><b>What to carry.</b> Tulasi garlands or flowers bought outside, small notes for the hundi and the priests' plate, a cloth bag for prasadam, and this app's diary to note the Lord's name before you leave the mandapam.</li>
<li><b>Sathari and tirtha.</b> The priest places the Lord's feet (sathari) on your head and gives three sips of water. Receive with the right hand cupped over the left.</li>
<li><b>Etiquette.</b> Do not pay touts for quick darshan. Ask before photographing; cameras are barred near most sanctums.</li>
</ul>`},
{id:'quizlink',title:'Test yourself: Learn the 108',html:`<p>A short quiz drawn from the app\'s own data: temples, postures, Thayars, Azhwars. Each temple you answer correctly counts as learnt.</p><p><a class="bigbtn" href="#quiz">Start the quiz</a></p>`},
{id:'gloss',title:'Glossary',html:`
<dl>
<dt>Azhwar (Alvar)</dt><dd>"One who is immersed" in God. The twelve poet-saints.</dd>
<dt>Pasuram</dt><dd>A verse of the Divya Prabandham.</dd>
<dt>Mangalasasanam</dt><dd>Singing for the Lord's well-being; an Azhwar's hymn on a shrine.</dd>
<dt>Perumal, Emperuman</dt><dd>"The great One", "our Lord": Vishnu.</dd>
<dt>Thayar, Nachiyar, Piratti</dt><dd>The Mother: Lakshmi in Her shrine.</dd>
<dt>Moolavar and Utsavar</dt><dd>The fixed main deity and the processional deity.</dd>
<dt>Nindra, Irundha, Kidantha</dt><dd>Standing, sitting and reclining postures of the Lord.</dd>
<dt>Vimanam</dt><dd>The tower over the sanctum. Gopuram is the gateway tower.</dd>
<dt>Pushkarini, Theertham</dt><dd>The sacred tank or water of a temple.</dd>
<dt>Sthala purana</dt><dd>The sacred history of a place.</dd>
<dt>Pratyaksham</dt><dd>The person to whom the Lord showed Himself at that place.</dd>
<dt>Sathari</dt><dd>The crown bearing the Lord's feet, placed on the pilgrim's head; identified with Nammazhwar.</dd>
<dt>Araiyar sevai</dt><dd>Sung and gestured performance of the Prabandham by hereditary temple singers.</dd>
<dt>Garuda Sevai</dt><dd>Procession of the Lord on His eagle carrier.</dd>
<dt>Brahmotsavam</dt><dd>The main annual festival, usually nine or ten days.</dd>
<dt>Abhimana sthalam</dt><dd>A revered Vishnu temple not sung by the Azhwars, such as Guruvayur, Udupi, Melkote, Sriperumbudur or Srimushnam.</dd>
<dt>Kainkarya</dt><dd>Loving service; the goal of life for a Sri Vaishnava.</dd>
<dt>Prapatti, Saranagati</dt><dd>Surrender; taking the Lord as one's only refuge.</dd>
</dl>`}
];

